---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="06-string-algos"
  lessonSlug="03-find-first"
  hook="Think Python's first example of `return` inside a loop. Search pattern with early exit."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def find_letter(word, letter):
    for i in range(len(word)):
        if word[i] == letter:
            return i         # found it — exit immediately
    return -1                # not found

print(find_letter("banana", "a"))    # 1
print(find_letter("banana", "z"))    # -1
print(find_letter("", "a"))          # -1`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Source: Think Python §8.6">
    <p>
      <em>"This is the first example we have seen of a return statement
      inside a loop. If word[index] == letter, the function breaks out of
      the loop and returns immediately."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The same shape — return the VALUE, not the index</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def first_word_with(words, letter):
    for w in words:
        if w.startswith(letter):
            return w
    return None

print(first_word_with(["the", "quick", "brown"], "q"))    # "quick"
print(first_word_with(["the", "quick", "brown"], "z"))    # None`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Don't put 'return -1' inside the loop's else">
    <p>
      A common bug: <code>return -1</code> inside an <code>else</code> branch
      makes the function bail after checking ONLY index 0. The
      "not found" return must be AFTER the loop.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
